WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.530
Speaker 1: Welcome everybody, I want to start
today with the worker strikes that have been

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:10.890
really spreading nationally across a variety
of different industries and types of jobs,

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.700
and I think it's very interesting what's going
on.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.820
There's a couple of different perspectives
about why this is taking place if and why

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.789
this is the right time for these types of
strikes.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:28.890
But the big picture for me is this is a good
thing without weighing in on the reasonableness

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.140
or the specifics of every strike.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:38.370
Large employers are faced with the reality
that for whatever reason, you believe there

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:44.350
are so many open jobs and we're going to talk
about that in a moment that employees or potential

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:51.130
employees or former employees have what appears
to be some newfound leverage, which hopefully

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.850
they can use to their advantage.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:57.210
So let's start first with a Washington Post
article.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:02.720
Strikes are sweeping the labor market as workers
wield new leverage.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.390
The labor activism runs the gamut of American
industry, powered by the same grievances about

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.609
wages, benefits and quality of life.

00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:11.890
Driving the great resignation.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.829
Let's look at a little more of this.

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:17.729
Americans are leaving their jobs at staggering
rates.

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:21.659
A record 4.3 million quit in August alone.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:26.009
Hundreds of thousands of workers with similar
grievances about wages, benefits and quality

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:29.340
of life are choosing to dig in and fight.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.420
Last week, 10000 John Deere workers went on
strike.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.329
Unions representing 31000 Kaiser employees
authorized walkouts.

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:43.750
60000 Hollywood production workers reached
a deal Saturday night, averting a strike.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.170
Hours before a negotiation deadline.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:53.450
All told, there have been strikes against
178 employers this year, according to a tracker

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.860
by Cornell University's School of Industrial
and Labor Relations.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.899
The Bureau of Labor Statistics, which records
only large work stoppages, has documented

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:09.440
12 strikes involving a thousand or more workers
so far this year.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.710
That's higher than 2020, when the pandemic
took hold in line with strike activity from

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.950
2019 and from 2018 the trend.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.890
Union officials and economists say, is an
offshoot of the phenomenon known as the great

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:27.790
resignation, which has thinned the nation's
labor pool and slowed economic recovery.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.900
Workers are now harder to replace, especially
while many companies are scrambling to meet

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.550
heightened demand for products and manage
hobbled supply chains that has given unions

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:40.430
new leverage and made striking less risky.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.820
This is a really important thing to understand.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:52.530
The one story that is essentially being told
is the reason there are open jobs and the

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:57.800
reason why so many restaurants, for example,
are putting up signs saying, We've got no

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.080
employees, we've got to close.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.170
Is lazy workers choosing to sit at home collecting
enhanced unemployment?

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:11.730
Now this is not only wrong, it's also incomplete
and it's also deceptive, first of all.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:15.770
The enhanced unemployment is all but completely
gone and phased out.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.760
So yes, there are people collecting unemployment.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:24.990
The vast majority are collecting what is standard
in the state in which they in which they live.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:25.990
And that varies.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.070
And in some states, it's really not very much.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:36.600
The second piece of this is that the child
care component is a particularly big factor

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.480
because of the concerns about COVID.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.410
If you have kids that are too young to be
vaccinated, it's not just a financial consideration,

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:46.050
it's also what about my kids who can't be
vaccinated?

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.250
Do I want them in school or not or part time?

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.350
So the school piece is a big piece of this.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:04:01.430
We also have this additional wrinkle, which
is anecdotally, I've had people write to me

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.830
and take this for what it is, which is just
an anecdote I'm being upfront about that people

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:09.270
have written to me and they've said, Listen,
David, my employer.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.990
Claims to be hiring.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:16.630
So when you hear about 100 unfilled positions,
a couple of people wrote to me from different

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:21.970
companies saying my employer has 20 positions
listed, 30 positions listed, 40 positions

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.250
listed.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.380
We're not interviewing anybody for these positions.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:32.730
And what these folks writing to me believe
is going on is that the companies are listing

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:39.630
jobs in order to placate the workers they
have who have been given the job of one and

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:44.880
a half people or two people, in some cases
during the pandemic, so that the workers believe

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.280
we're going to replace those folks who left
or who we laid off or whatever the case may

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.280
be.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.840
We're hiring, but really, it's to keep the
existing employees doing the jobs of one and

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.190
a half or two people calm as they continue
to do way more work than they normally would,

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:04.150
because the company would say, Hey, we'd love
to just have one person doing a job we used

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:08.080
to need, you know, three people for two jobs
or two people for whatever the case may be.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.010
So anecdotally, that's going on.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:16.720
And then also, the leverage is coming from
the particularly acute need from a lot of

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.950
these companies to actually scale up logistics
companies, delivery companies, a lot of products.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:26.240
There's a couple of products I'm waiting for
that are just heavily.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.760
I don't buy a lot of stuff, but the couple
of things I'm actually trying to buy right

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:30.760
now.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:34.230
I'm told it's going to be a couple of months
before we can even get them to you.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:40.110
And that is giving employees and unions more
leverage than they would normally have.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.340
So there's a lot of good here.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:48.790
It's it's not good to have a supply chain
that doesn't feel like it's functioning properly

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:53.139
and to have an inability to access basic products,
that's not good.

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:56.620
That is less related to what we're talking
about here.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:04.060
It's a good thing for labor and workers to
have a little bit more leverage disproportionately.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.650
They tend not to have it.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:10.230
There are a ton of so-called best jobs out
there right now, and a big part of that is

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:16.760
employee or employers first finding ways to
nickel and dime employees who are W-2 employees,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.760
then shifting to a model of just putting people
as independent contractors to 199.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.669
That's a big topic of controversy with Uber
and similar companies, and I've talked about

00:06:25.669 --> 00:06:26.800
this before.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:28.280
It it is.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:32.800
I mean, listen, I don't think at this point
I could work for someone else.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:39.639
I might be functionally unemployable at this
point if I had to go and apply for a job and

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:43.920
have a boss who gives me a schedule and monitors
breaks and all these different things.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.880
I think that I've been working for myself
for so long at this point that I would not

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:50.880
even be able to.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.870
I think it would be terrible.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:57.070
And when I speak to friends who are mostly
in that position, most of the people I know

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:59.780
are not working for themselves.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.250
It sounds absolutely terrible for a lot.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:03.250
Not all of them.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.210
A lot of them have good jobs, so anything
that can be done to regain some leverage is

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:08.210
great.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:12.920
And it appears as though at least some of
these strikes are are helping do that now.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.640
By the way, if you're striking but you didn't
vote for candidates, you could have voted

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:24.010
for either locally state federal president
who ran on higher minimum wage democratization

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:25.940
of the workplace, more benefits.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:29.590
You should be examining that as well, because
in addition to striking, you could also be

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:33.730
voting for the changes that you want as well
as a boss.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.840
I try to do all the things I can reasonably
do as a small employer.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:42.710
We have, you know, we condense the production
of the show into four days.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:43.740
There's no set schedules.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.160
We have an open vacation policy.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.570
We just make sure everybody's work is covered.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:52.090
I split health insurance 50-50 with some employers
do more, some do less.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:53.790
We're able to do 50 50.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.840
We do retirement account contributions.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.310
As a small employer, I'm trying to do what
I can.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.370
Large employers are in dramatically different
situations.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:09:23.530
I think what's going on is very interesting
and we'll see what results come from it.

